# HOW THIS FILE IS RENDERED BY THE CARGO PRODUCT BUILD
#
# This is the pkg-config template, and it now has three renderers rather
# than two. Autotools substitutes it through AC_SUBST and config.status;
# CMake substitutes it with configure_file() in @ONLY mode at
# CMakeLists.txt:2326-2328. Both remain in the tree and remain exercised,
# because the C build is retained as the reference oracle this rewrite is
# measured against; what changed is that neither of them renders the
# PRODUCT any more.
#
# The product renderer is curl-rs-ffi/build.rs, and a Cargo build runs
# neither Autotools nor CMake to reach it: the build script reads this
# file, fills in the fourteen substitution tokens and writes
# lib/pkgconfig/libcurl.pc, mode 0644, into an install-staging directory
# under target/ -- or wherever CURL_RS_STAGING_DIR names, and never into
# the checkout.
#
# The token spelling is deliberately unchanged. Both CMake's
# configure_file() and the new renderer understand it, so a packager
# diffing this template against curl 8.x sees added comments and nothing
# else. Those comments therefore name each token WITHOUT its surrounding
# at-signs: substitution is a plain global replacement in both renderers,
# so a real token written inside a comment would be replaced there too.
#
# The pkg-config contract is frozen, not merely preserved. Every field
# name below, and the order of the fields, is the one curl 8.x published.
# Consumers run `pkg-config --libs libcurl`, `--cflags libcurl`,
# `--modversion libcurl`, `--variable=supported_protocols libcurl` and
# `--variable=supported_features libcurl`, and every one of them must keep
# answering in the same shape now that a Rust library stands behind it.
#
# The install layout. prefix is the only externally supplied value: the
# renderer reads CURL_RS_PREFIX and otherwise falls back to the same
# /usr/local Autotools defaulted to. The other three are pkg-config
# variable references resolved against it, so relocating the package needs
# no edit here. Only that namespaced variable is consulted -- a bare
# PREFIX inherited from an unrelated tool would silently describe install
# paths libcurl was never installed to.
prefix=@prefix@
exec_prefix=@exec_prefix@
libdir=@libdir@
includedir=@includedir@
# The two variables a consumer reads back with `pkg-config --variable=`.
#
# SUPPORT_PROTOCOLS expands to the schemes this build really serves, space
# separated and upper-cased exactly as curl 8.x spells them here. The
# TARGET SCOPE is nine schemes and no more -- FILE FTP FTPS HTTP HTTPS SCP
# SFTP WS WSS -- but scope is not the expansion. The expansion is whatever
# the capability table reports as served, and measured at this checkpoint
# that is NONE of the nine, because no scheme has a protocol executor yet.
# So this variable currently expands to the EMPTY string and
# `pkg-config --variable=supported_protocols libcurl` prints a blank line.
# That is the truthful report of a library that can parse a URL and cannot
# fetch it, and it is the same answer the Protocols: line of
# `curl --version` gives, from the same table. It becomes the nine, without
# an edit here, as each executor lands.
#
# THIS FIELD DESCRIBES THE LIBRARY, SO IPFS AND IPNS ARE NOT IN IT, NOW OR
# EVER. Those two tokens are the curl TOOL's own
# (src/tool_libinfo.c:47-48, carried in curl-rs/src/cli/libinfo.rs): the
# tool inserts them into the Protocols: line of `curl --version` so a
# gateway URL can be translated on the command line. The engine registers
# no ipfs and no ipns scheme among its 33 rows, so a libcurl consumer
# cannot request one, and pkg-config metadata that named them would be
# advertising a library capability that does not exist. curl-config
# --protocols is silent about them for the same reason.
#
# The twenty-four schemes that remain registered for ABI completeness but
# are not implemented -- DICT, GOPHER, GOPHERS, IMAP, IMAPS, LDAP, LDAPS,
# MQTT, MQTTS, POP3, POP3S, RTMP and its variants, RTSP, SMB, SMBS, SMTP,
# SMTPS, TELNET and TFTP -- are deliberately absent, and that absence is
# the point rather than an oversight: they are out of scope for
# implementation but not for the ABI, so their absence has to be reported
# honestly. MQTTS is named beside MQTT deliberately; the registry carries
# both mqtt and mqtts, and listing only one would leave the enumeration
# short of the twenty-four it counts. 283 fixtures -- 14.8% of the corpus
# -- depend on this omission to skip cleanly. A request for one of them is
# specified to return CURLE_UNSUPPORTED_PROTOCOL. These twenty-four are
# absent permanently, which is what separates them from the nine above:
# the nine are withheld only until their engine lands.
#
# SUPPORT_FEATURES expands to the truthful subset of curl's own feature
# vocabulary, each name spelled as configure.ac spells it from :5143
# onward and the list sorted case-insensitively as CMakeLists.txt:2040-2045
# sorts it: SSL, IPv6, UnixSockets, libz, brotli, zstd, AsynchDNS, IDN,
# PSL, alt-svc, HSTS, NTLM, HTTP2, HTTP3, Largefile, threadsafe and their
# neighbours. That is the vocabulary, not a reading: a name is emitted only
# when this build genuinely has the capability, which at this checkpoint
# excludes most of them. The rendered value is short today -- IDN,
# Largefile and threadsafe at the time of writing -- because the engine
# rows behind SSL, HTTP2, HTTP3, NTLM, alt-svc, HSTS and the rest are still
# absent. Treat that as a snapshot and ask the rendered file instead, which
# is the only answer that cannot be stale:
#   pkg-config --variable=supported_features libcurl
#
# Withheld on purpose, because each one would be a claim a test harness
# then acts on:
#
#   Debug, and with it TrackMemory -- advertising Debug arms the harness's
#   allocation-cap and leak accounting against a Rust allocator whose
#   allocation pattern cannot match a C one. The cost of withholding it is
#   stated rather than hidden: 98 fixtures skip and torture-test does not
#   apply.
#
#   Every name belonging to a dropped backend, since rustls, quinn and
#   russh are the sole implementations: MultiSSL, OpenSSL, GnuTLS,
#   mbedtls, wolfssl, Schannel, SSPI, TLS-SRP, NTLM_WB, WinIDN, AppleIDN,
#   libssh, libssh2, c-ares and gsasl.
#
#   ...but NOT GSS-API, SPNEGO or Kerberos. Those three are compiled in
#   only behind the non-default `negotiate` Cargo feature, and when they
#   are compiled in this file names them -- because the question it
#   answers is what this libcurl was BUILT to do, and that is the
#   question the feature gate already decides. C answers it the same way:
#   configure.ac:5175-5200 appends the three names to SUPPORT_FEATURES
#   from HAVE_GSSAPI, a build-time fact it never re-examines.
#
#   Whether a usable mechanism exists on the HOST is a different question,
#   and it is genuinely a runtime one that a file written at build time
#   cannot answer. It is left to `curl --version`, which can ask, and
#   whose banner conjoins that probe before naming the token. Only the
#   banner is read by tests/runtests.pl, so the two surfaces answering
#   two different questions costs no fixture anything.
#
# Neither list is maintained here, and neither is maintained in the
# renderer. Both are projections of the one capability table in
# curl-rs-lib/src/version.rs that is also specified to render the `curl
# --version` banner, so this file and that banner cannot describe different
# products -- which matters because tests/runtests.pl parses the banner's
# Protocols: line and derives the -ipv6 and -unix variants, http-proxy and
# https-mtls from it. A scheme appears here only once the engine module
# that serves it is present, so the list is never ahead of the code.
#
# One half of that shared projection is not observable yet, and saying so
# is the difference between a contract and a claim: the banner PRINTER
# (curl-rs/src/cli/help.rs, from src/tool_help.c:311-386) is not on disk,
# and the executable currently rejects every option including --version, so
# no banner is emitted at all. This file and curl-config are therefore the
# only two surfaces reading that table today. They agree with each other,
# which is checkable now; agreement with the banner becomes checkable when
# the printer lands.
supported_protocols="@SUPPORT_PROTOCOLS@"
supported_features="@SUPPORT_FEATURES@"

Name: libcurl
URL: https://curl.se/
Description: Library to transfer files with HTTP, FTP, etc.
# LIBCURL_VERSION of include/curl/curlver.h, not bumped here: the version is
# part of the frozen ABI contract.
Version: @CURLVERSION@
# Both expand to EMPTY, which is truthful rather than an omission: pkg-config
# fails outright when a name in Requires cannot be resolved, and every C
# library curl used to require is now a statically linked Rust crate with no
# .pc file. CMakeLists.txt:2144 and :2260 leave both empty as well.
Requires: @LIBCURL_PC_REQUIRES@
Requires.private: @LIBCURL_PC_REQUIRES_PRIVATE@
# -lcurl must never change, which is why curl-rs-ffi/Cargo.toml builds under
# [lib] name = "curl": the artifacts really are libcurl.so and libcurl.a. The
# shared object carries DT_SONAME = libcurl.so.4, derived rather than chosen
# -- lib/Makefile.soname gives libtool -version-info 12:0:8, and the soname
# major is current - age = 12 - 8 = 4. build.rs emits it as a cdylib-scoped
# link argument (an -install_name form on macOS) because rustflags are not
# artifact-scoped and were measured to stamp it on the two binaries too.
Libs: -L${libdir} -lcurl @LIBCURL_PC_LIBS@
# What a consumer adds when linking the static archive: the platform C
# runtime, plus the GSS library when the `negotiate` feature is on. The order
# is the one a left-to-right static linker needs, so -lc is last. The same
# authority renders `curl-config --static-libs`, so the two cannot disagree.
Libs.private: @LIBCURL_PC_LDFLAGS_PRIVATE@ @LIBCURL_PC_LIBS_PRIVATE@
# includedir must HOLD the curl subdirectory rather than be it, because the
# public headers are included as <curl/curl.h> -- see include/README.md.
Cflags: -I${includedir} @LIBCURL_PC_CFLAGS@
# -DCURL_STATICLIB, as CMakeLists.txt:2255 sets it: a consumer linking the
# static archive must define it so the public headers do not decorate the API
# for import from a shared library.
Cflags.private: @LIBCURL_PC_CFLAGS_PRIVATE@
